TaskVM — W1 Kill-Test Implementation Plan
Context
Why this change. TaskVM is a CHI 2027 full paper (deadline ≈ 2026-09-10 AoE). The project compiles live state of multiple existing running apps into an editable, executable, verifiable task interface; a user edits one task variable; an agent writes the change back across apps; an independent verifier reads hidden ground-truth state and judges "changed happened / unchanged untouched / interface re-synced." W1 (gate, not milestone) is a kill-test: prove the compiler→UI→patch→execute→verifier chain runs end-to-end on 2 apps (Calendar+TaskBoard) in replay-mode, or shrink/stop per the three sub-kills. All 15+1 product/research decisions are locked in docs/A2UI_开工大纲_v0_心智模型对齐版.md (单一权威文档·锁定版); this plan is pure engineering.

Time reality (revised per user feedback). Handoff says "today 2026-07-30," but the live system date is 2026-08-05. Per user direction: do NOT compress W1 to a 1-day sprint to make 8/6. Schedule by what the kill-test actually needs — 2 tasks + the complete sub-kill judgment — and take as many days as that genuinely requires. The CHI 2027 timeline is subordinate to making the demo + verification chain solid: a delay is acceptable; a false-passing gate is not. The 2-task minimum is the core defense against sub-kill-3 ("only hand-written binding works → custom dashboard, not a compiler") and must not be quietly skipped for time. Other W1-scope choices (in-memory not sqlite, hand-authored trace not live capture, rule-based patch_compiler, structured-text UI, JSON-parse + binding-schema validation not full L1) are scope-driven (these are W2+ features the kill-test does not need), NOT time-driven shortcuts.

Intended outcome. When W1 completes: evaluation/run_w1_killtest.py runs 2 fixtures × N≥3 compiler samples, prints round-trip score + per-check pass rates + binding-accuracy, and a negative-control run (broken dispatcher) scores ≤0.3 — proving the verifier is honest. A PASS greenlights W2; a fail triggers the documented sub-kill.

