Three load-bearing invariants — violating any one voids the kill-test:

Read-path-is-GUI / write-path-is-API split (from Plan-agent pressure-test). The compiler reads the apps via rendered GUI observations (screenshot + DOM + a11y text captured from the live HTML) — this is what makes "compile live app state" faithful. The executor writes via the apps' own app-level API (reliable, no CUA flakiness in W1). NEVER let the compiler read the DB/session directly — that collapses the projection claim into a DB viewer. Live CUA GUI driving on the write path = W2 escalation, not a W1 faithfulness requirement (apps are real Flask apps with own state; app-API = how MCP/tool agents work).

No-leak canonical state (mirror SenseAct verbatim). Canonical task graph + expected state-diff live only in benchmark/fixtures.py, injected server-side via POST /api/inject_task/<sid> into user_sessions[sid]. reset() returns only task-info (goal/task_id/session_id/start_url). /api/session_state/<sid> returns only a summary (counts/status). The compiler sees ONLY screenshot/DOM/a11y/tool-schema/ trajectory — never the DB, never oracle_answer, never the canonical graph. Oracle read only inside done/check_state/verifier.

Negative-control test (honesty safeguard). Every kill-test run is paired with a run whose action_dispatcher is intentionally broken (no-op / wrong target). That run MUST score ≤0.3. If it doesn't, the verifier is broken — the kill-test is void until fixed. This is the single most important defense against verifier false-positives.

What W1 tests vs. what it does NOT (positioning — per user feedback). The kill-test's gate-critical claim is the model's binding discovery: can a frontier model, given only rendered app observations (screenshot/DOM/a11y/tool-schema), compile a correct typed task-state graph + binding (task_state/compiler.py)? The PASS criterion "no hand-written binding" exists precisely to test this. By contrast, execution/patch_compiler.py's patch generation — applying a user edit to the now-fixed binding's known operators — is a deterministic engineering step; rule-based is fine, it is NOT what is being tested. The final W1 report must state this distinction explicitly, so reviewers do not mistake the kill-test as measuring rule-system capability rather than the model's binding-discovery capability.

