2. Calendar + TaskBoard minimal resettable Web apps
Clone SenseAct scenarios/calendar/app.py shape. In-memory only for W1 (no sqlite — sqlite is W3 scale; the Plan-agent MVP confirms in-memory suffices). Per app:

user_sessions: dict + threading.RLock; fresh random sid per episode; reap_sessions(max=256) FIFO cap; tick_session step counter (port senseact/web_helpers.py).
Routes (mirror SenseAct contract): GET /<sid> (HTML view), GET /api/<resource>/<sid> (JSON list), POST /api/event/<sid>/move & POST /api/task/<sid>/<tid> (mutate + append to sess["actions"]), POST /api/inject_task/<sid> (seed), GET /api/session_state/<sid> (summary only), POST /api/reset/<sid>, GET /health.
Calendar state: events: [{eid, title, date, time, calendar, rsvp}]. Mutate op = move_event(eid, new_date) (the release-meeting move).
TaskBoard state: tasks: [{tid, title, status, assignee, deadline, depends_on: []}] where depends_on references a task variable (e.g. release_date). Mutate op = set_deadline(tid, new_date) + set_status(tid, status).
HTML templates expose stable DOM IDs: every event row carries data-event-id="E1", every task row data-task-id="T3" — so the compiler can extract entity IDs from DOM/a11y (the #1 reliability risk). This is the read-path-GUI surface.
engine/reward.py: harmonized get_reward(submitted, goal, verbose=False) -> (float, dict) + check_state(goal, verbose=False) -> (float, dict), info shape {oracle_type, exact, submitted, checks, total}. W1 oracle_types: event_moved, deadline_synced (state-mode, read sess["actions"]).
engine/injector.py: SenseAct-style inject(state, task_data) -> state; reserves a no-op inject_external_change() hook for W4+ concurrent-injection (doc §15-E-Q11).
3. Canonical task graph: hiding + reading
benchmark/fixtures.py holds CanonicalTaskGraph (the GT binding + expected post-edit state-diff), porting SenseAct's tasks/task_suites/<scenario>/tasks.json fixture format ({task_id, goal, oracle_answer, task_data, budget_tokens}) and extending it:

@dataclass
class CanonicalBinding:
    var_id: str                      # "release_date"
    app: str                          # "calendar" | "taskboard"
    entity_id: str                    # "E1" | "T1"
    field: str                        # "date" | "deadline"
    operator: str                     # "move_event" | "set_deadline"
    expected_value_after_edit: Any    # GT for verifier

@dataclass
class CanonicalTaskGraph:
    task_id: str
    goal: str
    seed_state: dict                  # calendar events + taskboard tasks at t0
    user_edit: dict                   # {var_id:"release_date", old:"2026-08-14", new:"2026-08-18"}
    bindings: list[CanonicalBinding]  # GT: release_date → E1.move_event + T1.set_deadline + T2.set_deadline
    non_interference_set: list[str]   # entity_ids that must NOT change (e.g. unrelated meetings E7, tasks T9)
    expected_diff: dict               # per-app expected post-edit state, for "changed-happened"
Hiding: inject_task puts seed_state into both apps' user_sessions[sid]; the CanonicalTaskGraph itself stays in fixtures.py and is passed ONLY to the verifier — never to the compiler. Reading: verifier/canonical_state.py snapshots both apps' real session state via state_adapter.read_canonical(sid) and compares against expected_diff + non_interference_set.

No-coupling boundary (per user feedback — the no-leak principle's most fragile point). task_state/entity_binding.py::OPERATOR_REGISTRY is compiler-visible and must contain ONLY "what operations exist" — function signatures (move_event(eid, new_date), set_deadline(tid, new_date), set_status(tid, status)) with no var_id mappings. benchmark/fixtures.py::CanonicalBinding.operator is verifier-only GT and carries the specific "which var_id binds to which operator" mapping. No hardcoded coupling between these two files: the registry must not name var_ids; fixtures.py must not be importable by the compiler path (task_state/, execution/). This boundary is the easiest place to accidentally leak GT into the compiler — it gets an explicit implementation check AND a dedicated code-review pass before W1 sign-off (see Verification step 6).

4. Replay engine + trace format
harness/replay_engine.py + benchmark/fixtures.py::TraceFixture. W1 scope (SenseAct has no replay engine — build fresh, but minimal): replay = the compiler's INPUT is a frozen, hand-authored observation set, not live-captured. trace_capture.py is a W2 stub (live capture); W1 hand-authors the trace:

@dataclass
class StepObservation:
    step: int
    screenshot_path: str              # PNG of the rendered app(s)
    dom_html: str                     # serialized DOM (with data-event-id/data-task-id)
    a11y_text: str                    # flattened AXTree text with [bid] ids
    action_taken: str | None          # the recorded action string (for trajectory)

@dataclass
class TraceFixture:
    task_id: str
    steps: list[StepObservation]      # 1–3 steps: setup actions that create the release meeting + tasks
    final_obs: StepObservation        # the state the compiler compiles FROM
replay_engine.load(task_id) -> TraceFixture returns the frozen observations; replay_engine.seed_apps(task_id, sid) calls both apps' inject_task to set seed_state. The EXECUTE + VERIFY steps are live (real app-API mutations, real canonical read) — only the compiler's input is replayed.

Replay/state consistency assert (per user feedback — protects the "live state" anchor). After replay_engine.seed_apps(task_id, sid), assert that the hand-authored final_obs (screenshot/DOM/a11y) is field-by-field consistent with the real session state read via state_adapter.read_canonical(sid) after inject_task — i.e. every entity in the canonical snapshot appears in the DOM with the same field values, and vice versa. This catches the hidden "chain passes but the compiler is looking at a static image detached from the current real session state" failure, which would silently void the "live state" anchor. Lives in replay_engine.assert_obs_matches_state(sid, final_obs) and runs before every compiler call; a mismatch fails the run loudly (not a soft warning).

