6. Verifier round-trip judgment
verifier/round_trip_checks.py + verifier/non_interference.py + verifier/canonical_state.py. Pure Python, reads canonical state only. Three checks → score in [0,1] + info dict (mirror SenseAct reward info shape):

def check_round_trip(sid, fixture: CanonicalTaskGraph, apps: dict, pre_snapshot: dict) -> tuple[float, dict]:
    post = {name: ad.read_canonical(sid) for name, ad in apps.items()}
    changed = check_changed_happened(post, fixture.expected_diff)        # each binding's expected_value_after_edit met
    untouched = check_non_interference(post, fixture.non_interference_set, pre_snapshot)  # those entity_ids unchanged
    resynced = check_interface_resynced(sid, fixture, apps)              # re-compile UI from post-state, structurally matches the edited var
    # AOHP-style checkpoint-weighted score (doc §3 AOHP borrow): partial credit for partial cross-app success (no atomic txn, §14-6)
    score = 0.5*changed.fraction + 0.3*untouched.fraction + 0.2*resynced.fraction
    return score, {"changed": changed.info, "untouched": untouched.info, "resynced": resynced.info, "total": score}
changed-happened: for each CanonicalBinding, the app's real post-state at entity_id.field == expected_value_after_edit. Fraction = met/total.
non-interference (HARD constraint — if it fails, the run fails regardless of score): every entity_id in non_interference_set is byte-identical between pre and post snapshots. non_interference.fraction must be 1.0 for a PASS.
interface-re-synced (no model): re-run the renderer on the post-state canonical graph (NOT the compiler — the verifier uses the GT graph) and assert the edited var_id now shows the new value; structural match (the field is present + value updated). This checks the surface would re-sync, without calling the model.
