W1 exit criteria + sub-kill triggers
Run: 2 fixtures × N≥3 compiler samples (temp 0.3), frontier model on proxy. Each run paired with a negative-control (broken dispatcher). Report mean round-trip score + per-check pass rates + binding-accuracy + which-link-broke diagnostics.

PASS (greenlight W2):

≥1 of the 2 tasks achieves round-trip score ≥0.85 on ≥2/3 samples, AND
honest: negative-control scores ≤0.3, AND
non_interference = 1.0 on passing runs (hard fail otherwise), AND
passing task used NO hand-written binding (binding model-discovered from observations).
Sub-kill-1 (round-trip can't run → shrink to direction-2 typed cross-app operators): both tasks <0.5 across all samples, or chain can't complete end-to-end. Action: stop UI/model work; hand-bind OPERATOR_REGISTRY as a direction-2 system.

Sub-kill-2 (rules as good as model → train-free evidence): W1 only collects the data point (rule-based patch_compiler vs frontier patch_compiler on 2 tasks). Not a W1 stop; feeds the W4 Go/No-Go.

Sub-kill-3 (only hand-written binding works → STOP): the model-discovered binding never validates but a researcher-authored TaskBinding does, for BOTH tasks. Action: STOP — that's a custom dashboard, not a compiler. The 2-task minimum exists to detect this.

Honest-failure logging: every failing sample records compile_failure / binding_miss / dispatch_partial / verify_fail with raw model output + validation errors — the kill-test's diagnostic payload.

Verification (how to run the kill-test end-to-end)
docker compose up → Calendar :3013 + TaskBoard :3014 healthy (GET /health).
python -m taskvm.evaluation.run_w1_killtest --task release_reschedule --samples 3 --model gpt-5.5 seeds both apps per fixtures.py, replays the frozen trace into the compiler, renders the task surface, applies the scripted release_date 8/14→8/18 edit, dispatches via app-API, then runs the verifier.
Inspect eval_results/w1_<ts>.json: per-sample score, changed/untouched/resynced fractions, binding_accuracy, which_link_broke, and the neg-control score.
Unit-check each layer in isolation (build order above) before the full run: apps mutate+reset; state_adapter snapshot/compare; dependency_graph.propagate; compiler output validates; action_dispatcher mutates real state; verifier scores a known post-state correctly.
Negative-control: --neg-control flag breaks the dispatcher; assert score ≤0.3.
Code-review checkpoint (pre-W1 sign-off — per user feedback): explicitly verify (a) the OPERATOR_REGISTRY vs CanonicalBinding.operator no-coupling boundary — no var_ids in the registry, benchmark/fixtures.py not importable by the compiler path (task_state/, execution/); (b) the replay/state consistency assert (assert_obs_matches_state) fires before every compiler call and fails loudly on mismatch; (c) the compiler path has no import of benchmark.fixtures or verifier.*; (d) the negative-control genuinely breaks the dispatcher (a no-op that scores 0 for the right reason — non-interference passes but changed-happened fails — not a degenerate all-zero that passes the ≤0.3 threshold trivially).
Risks (concentrated, measurable, mitigated)
Compiler emits wrong/non-existent entity_id (biggest) — mitigated by stable data-event-id/data-task-id DOM IDs + validate_binding existence check + N≥3 samples. If frontier model can't extract IDs from DOM/a11y at >70%, sub-kill-1 looms.
Partial cross-app failure (no atomic txn, §14-6) — accepted as honest partial credit via AOHP-weighted scoring; never hidden.
Verifier false-positive — killed by the negative-control test (must score ≤0.3).
Sub-kill-3 (hand-written binding) — detected by the 2-task minimum.
Schedule — W1 takes as long as an honest 2-task + full-sub-kill pass requires (delay acceptable per user direction; false-pass not). Scope-driven stubs (sqlite/L1/trace_capture/patch_compiler/UI) are W2+ features the kill-test does not need, NOT time shortcuts; the 2-task minimum, model binding discovery, verifier, and negative-control are the un-stubbable core.
A2UI-spec completeness — live a2ui.org blocked; local prompts.tex:56-116 is sufficient for W1 (field lists + 6 rules + 15-component whitelist). Formal JSON Schema authored from field lists if needed; not gate-critical (binding+round-trip is the gate, not A2UI rendering fidelity).
Verdict: the design passes the kill-test conceptually — the chain is sound, the reliability risk is concentrated in one measurable failure mode (compiler binding accuracy) mitigated deterministically downstream, and the honesty safeguards (negative-control + no-leak + non-interference hard-fail) protect the claim. Whether it passes empirically depends on the frontier model's ability to extract stable entity IDs from rendered DOM/a11y — which is exactly what W1 exists to find out.

W1 scope (scope-driven, not time-driven — per user feedback)
In scope (must be real, no stubs): 2 tasks — the 2-task minimum is non-negotiable, it is the sub-kill-3 defense ("only hand-written binding works → custom dashboard, not a compiler"); model-discovered binding (task_state/compiler.py, live frontier call — the gate-critical claim, PASS requires "no hand-written binding"); app-API execution (real state change in both apps); verifier reading canonical state; negative-control test; the replay/state consistency assert; the OPERATOR_REGISTRY vs CanonicalBinding.operator no-coupling boundary.

Out of scope (W2+ features the kill-test does NOT need — these are scope decisions, not time shortcuts): sqlite (in-memory suffices for 2 apps / 2 tasks); full L1 5-sub-dim render-check (JSON-parse + binding-schema validation only); live trace_capture (hand-authored trace — replay-mode is by definition frozen input); frontier-model patch_compiler (rule-based — patch generation is deterministic engineering, NOT the gate claim); HTML form renderer (structured text only); all W2+ modules (replanner/shadow_txn/browser_controller/editable_components/live_sync/ reconciliation/cross_app — empty stubs with docstrings reserving the interface, per the PromptPatch/Beyond-Submit reservations in handoff §10).

If the chain is failing: the #1 debug lever is validate_binding failures — inspect entity_id misses → fix DOM IDs / a11y labels; unknown operator → sharpen the system prompt's operator vocabulary; model won't emit task_binding alongside a2ui → drop the A2UI output requirement and ask for task_binding JSON only (permitted since "fancy UI" is de-prioritized, doc §10). Take the time to get an honest pass — do NOT narrow to 1 task or hand-write a binding to make a date; that would void the kill-test.